Below is a quick checklist of everything you need (and a few “nice-to-haves”) before the OSM raster tiles actually appear on a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣  The **source** definition you already have is almost complete

```js
map.addSource('osm', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  // optional, but recommended
  tileSize: 256,          // default for OSM, keep it explicit
  scheme: 'xyz',          // default, but good to be explicit
  maxzoom: 19,            // OSM tiles are served up to z=19
  minzoom: 0,
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors',
  // If you ever need CORS (e.g. for canvas export) you can set:
  // crossOrigin: 'anonymous',
});
```

**What you *might* be missing**

| Property | Why it matters | Typical value |
|----------|----------------|---------------|
| `tileSize` | Guarantees the correct pixel size (256 px for OSM). | `256` |
| `maxzoom` / `minzoom` | Prevents the map from requesting tiles that don’t exist (OSM stops at 19). | `19` / `0` |
| `attribution` | Required by the OSM Tile Usage Policy. | OSM attribution string |
| `crossOrigin` | Needed if you ever want to read the canvas (e.g., `map.getCanvas().toDataURL()`). | `'anonymous'` |
| `bounds` (optional) | Limits the source to the world extent; useful for debugging. | `[-180, -85.051129, 180, 85.051129]` |

> **Tip:** If you plan to use the tiles in a production app, consider hosting your own tile server or using a third-party tile provider (Mapbox, MapTiler, etc.). The public `tile.openstreetmap.org` endpoint is meant for low-traffic, non-commercial use and enforces a rate-limit of ~2 req s⁻¹ per IP.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a **layer** that points at the source

A raster source does nothing on its own – you need a raster layer (or a background layer) that tells MapLibre *how* to draw it.

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-raster',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm',          // matches the source id above
  paint: {
    // Optional: control opacity, fade, etc.
    // 'raster-opacity': 1,
    // 'raster-fade-duration': 0,
  }
});
```

If you already have a style JSON (e.g., a dark theme) you can insert the layer anywhere in the `layers` array where you want the OSM tiles to appear (usually **below** any vector layers you want to overlay).

---

## 3️⃣  Make sure the **map style** includes the source & layer

If you are building the style programmatically (as above) you’re fine. If you are loading a static style JSON, you need to add the source and layer objects to that JSON before you call `map = new maplibregl.Map({style: myStyle, …})`.

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 19,
      "attribution": "&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors"
    },
    "... other sources ..."
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "osm-raster",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "osm"
    },
    "... other layers ..."
  ]
}
```

---

## 4️⃣  Add an **AttributionControl** (optional but recommended)

MapLibre does not automatically show the source attribution unless you add the control yourself.

```js
map.addControl(
  new maplibregl.AttributionControl({
    compact: true,
    customAttribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
  })
);
```

If you already set `attribution` on the source, the control will pick it up automatically; the `customAttribution` option is just a safety net.

---

## 5️⃣  Verify **CORS** and **HTTPS**

* The OSM tile endpoint is HTTPS-only, so your page must also be served over HTTPS (or `localhost`).
* The tiles are served with the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` header, so they work out-of-the-box for most MapLibre use-cases. If you ever need to read pixel data from the canvas, keep `crossOrigin: 'anonymous'` in the source definition.

---

## 6️⃣  Test it!

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',          // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {},   // we’ll add sources later via addSource()
    layers: []     // same for layers
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

map.on('load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ source
  map.addSource('osm', {
    type: 'raster',
    tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
    tileSize: 256,
    maxzoom: 19,
    attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
  });

  // 2️⃣ layer
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'osm-raster',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'osm'
  });

  // 3️⃣ attribution UI
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.AttributionControl({compact: true}));
});
```

If you open the page and see the familiar OSM “gray-scale” map, you’re good to go.

---

## 📋 Quick “cheat-sheet” of the minimal working snippet

```js
map.addSource('osm', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '&copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm'
});

map.addControl(new maplibregl.AttributionControl({compact: true}));
```

That’s all you need for a **basic** OSM raster layer.

---

### 🎯 Bottom line

* **Source** – you already have it, just add the optional fields (`tileSize`, `maxzoom`, `attribution`, etc.).
* **Layer** – you must create a raster layer that points to that source.
* **Attribution** – add the control (or rely on the source’s `attribution` field) to stay compliant with the OSM Tile Usage Policy.
* **Rate-limit / usage** – the public OSM tile server is for low-traffic, non-commercial use only. For anything beyond that, spin up your own tile server or use a commercial provider.

Once those pieces are in place, the tiles will render correctly. Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a quick checklist of everything you need (and a few “nice-to-haves”) before the OSM raster tiles actually appear on a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣  Add the **source** to the style

```js
map.addSource('osm-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    // The classic OSM “XYZ” endpoint
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  // Optional, but recommended
  tileSize: 256,          // OSM tiles are 256 px (default, but explicit is clearer)
  scheme: 'xyz',          // default for OSM, keep it explicit
  maxzoom: 19,            // OSM serves up to z=19 (or 18 in some regions)
  minzoom: 0,
  // If you ever need to set CORS headers yourself:
  // crossOrigin: 'anonymous',
  // attribution is **not** a MapLibre property – you’ll add it to the UI yourself (see §4)
});
```

> **Why the explicit `tileSize` / `scheme`?**  
> The defaults happen to match OSM, but being explicit protects you if you ever copy-paste the block into a different style where the defaults may have been overridden.

---

## 2️⃣  Add a **layer** that points at that source

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-raster-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-raster',
  // Optional raster-specific paint properties
  paint: {
    // “raster-opacity” defaults to 1.0
    // 'raster-opacity': 0.9,
    // 'raster-fade-duration': 0   // disables the fade-in animation
  }
});
```

> **Order matters** – if you have other raster or background layers, make sure this layer is added **above** the background but **below** any vector layers you want on top (e.g., labels, markers). You can control that with the `before` argument of `addLayer` or by setting `layout: { 'visibility': 'visible' }` later.

---

## 3️⃣  Make sure the **map view** is in a zoom range where OSM actually has tiles

```js
map.setCenter([0, 0]);   // lon, lat
map.setZoom(2);          // any zoom 0-19 will work
```

If you start the map at a zoom > 19 you’ll just see a blank (or “no data”) tile because OSM doesn’t serve beyond that level.

---

## 4️⃣  **Attribution** – OSM’s tile usage policy

MapLibre does **not** automatically display an attribution string for raster sources, so you have to add it yourself (e.g., via a custom control, a simple HTML `<div>`, or the built-in `MapLibreGL.AttributionControl`).

```js
// Add the default attribution control (if you haven’t already)
map.addControl(new maplibregl.AttributionControl({
  compact: true,
  customAttribution: [
    // The required OSM credit
    '<a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright" target="_blank">© OpenStreetMap contributors</a>'
  ].join(' | ')
}));
```

> **Policy reminder** – OSM tiles are free to use for low-traffic projects, but you must:
> * Include the attribution exactly as shown above.
> * Cache responsibly (no aggressive pre-fetching, respect the `Cache-Control` header).
> * Keep request rates reasonable (≤ 2 req/s per IP is a good rule of thumb).  
> If you expect higher traffic, consider using a third-party tile provider (Mapbox, Thunderforest, etc.) or host your own tile cache.

---

## 5️⃣  (Optional) **Performance / UX tweaks**

| Setting | Why it helps | Example |
|---------|--------------|---------|
| `raster-fade-duration: 0` | Removes the default 300 ms fade-in, which can look jittery when you pan fast. | `paint: { 'raster-fade-duration': 0 }` |
| `raster-resampling: 'linear'` | Controls how the raster is interpolated when zoomed. `linear` is smoother; `nearest` is crisper. | `paint: { 'raster-resampling': 'linear' }` |
| `maxzoom` / `minzoom` | Prevents the map from requesting tiles that don’t exist (e.g., zoom 20). | `maxzoom: 19` |
| `bounds` on the source | If you only need a subset of the world, you can limit requests. | `bounds: [-180, -85, 180, 85]` |
| `tileSize: 512` + `raster-fade-duration: 0` | Some providers (e.g., Mapbox) use 512 px tiles; OSM does **not**, so keep it at 256. | — |

---

## 6️⃣  Full Minimal Working Example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre + OSM raster tiles</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.1.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},   // start empty; we’ll add our raster source later
      layers: []     // same here
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // Add attribution control (required by OSM)
  map.addControl(new maplibregl.AttributionControl({
    compact: true,
    customAttribution: [
      '<a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright" target="_blank">© OpenStreetMap contributors</a>'
    ].join(' | ')
  }));

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ source
    map.addSource('osm-raster', {
      type: 'raster',
      tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
      tileSize: 256,
      scheme: 'xyz',
      maxzoom: 19
    });

    // 2️⃣ layer
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'osm-raster-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'osm-raster',
      paint: {
        // optional tweaks
        // 'raster-fade-duration': 0,
        // 'raster-opacity': 1
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open that file in a browser and you should see the classic OSM basemap rendered as a raster layer.

---

## TL;DR Checklist

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| ✅ | Add a **raster source** with `type: 'raster'`, `tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png']`, `tileSize: 256`, `scheme: 'xyz'`, `maxzoom: 19`. |
| ✅ | Add a **raster layer** that references the source (`source: 'osm-raster'`). |
| ✅ | Set an initial **center/zoom** that falls within 0-19. |
| ✅ | Add an **attribution control** (or custom HTML) with the required OSM credit. |
| ✅ | (Optional) Fine-tune `raster-fade-duration`, `raster-opacity`, `raster-resampling`, etc. |
| ✅ | Respect OSM’s tile-usage policy (rate-limit, caching, attribution). |

If you have all of the above in place, the OSM raster tiles will render correctly in MapLibre. Happy mapping! 🚀
